cards.title:
On a core with a 16 KiB data cache and 64-byte lines (32-bit `int`), which function is faster, and roughly what fraction of its accesses to `a` miss?

```c
#define N 512
static int a[N][N];

long sum_rows(void) {
    long s = 0;
    for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) for (int j = 0; j < N; j++) s += a[i][j];
    return s;
}
long sum_cols(void) {
    long s = 0;
    for (int j = 0; j < N; j++) for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) s += a[i][j];
    return s;
}
```

Rewrite this loop nest with **loop interchange** so the inner loop has stride 1, and explain why that is legal.

```c
for (int j = 0; j < N; j++)
    for (int i = 0; i < N; i++)
        a[i][j] *= 2;
```

cards.body:
Swap the two loops, with `for i` outside and `for j` inside. It is legal because each element is updated independently, so the order of the iterations does not change the result.

```c
for (int i = 0; i < N; i++)
    for (int j = 0; j < N; j++)
        a[i][j] *= 2;
```

The inner loop now walks along a row, one element at a time (4 bytes apart if `a` holds `int`), instead of down a column.

Watch out: Interchange is not always legal. If one iteration uses a value written by another, such as `a[i][j] = a[i-1][j+1] + 1`, changing the order changes the answer. Some compilers interchange loops at high optimization levels, but do not count on it.

cards.title:
Transposing with `b[j][i] = a[i][j]` makes one of the two arrays strided. `a` and `b` are N×N arrays of 32-bit ints. Fill in the largest power of two `B` for which one B×B tile of `a` and one B×B tile of `b` fit together in a 16 KiB cache.

```c
#define N 1024
#define B ____
for (int ii = 0; ii < N; ii += B)
  for (int jj = 0; jj < N; jj += B)
    for (int i = ii; i < ii + B; i++)
      for (int j = jj; j < jj + B; j++)
        b[j][i] = a[i][j];
```

This benchmark of a column-order sum can print `0 ms` when compiled with optimization. Why, and what is the fix?

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <time.h>
#define N 2048
static int a[N][N];
int main(void) {
    clock_t t0 = clock();
    long s = 0;
    for (int j = 0; j < N; j++)
        for (int i = 0; i < N; i++) s += a[i][j];
    printf("%ld ms\n", (long)((clock() - t0) * 1000 / CLOCKS_PER_SEC));
}
```

cards.body:
Make the data real and use the result: `a` is never written, so the compiler sees an all-zero array and a constant sum and deletes the loop.

```c
// the fix: fill the array at run time, time the loop, print s
for (int i = 0; i < N; i++)
    for (int j = 0; j < N; j++) a[i][j] = i ^ j;
// ...timed loop as before, then: printf("%ld\n", s);
```

Watch out: Printing `s` alone is not enough here. With the all-zero array the compiler still folds the sum to 0 and drops the loop, and a `volatile` sink has the same problem. We checked this: `-O2` still printed `0 ms`.

Example: With `-O2` the original program printed `0 ms` on one Mac laptop. With `-O0` it printed a real time, but unoptimized code does not represent the build you ship.

Tip: A trustworthy timing experiment also uses an array much larger than the cache, repeats the run and reports the minimum or median, changes only the layout between versions, and uses a hardware cycle counter where the core has one (for example `mcycle` on RISC-V). Always time the same build flags you will deploy.

cards.title:
On a POSIX system with an MMU, what do the two printed lines show?

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <sys/wait.h>
#include <unistd.h>
int main(void) {
    int x = 1;
    pid_t pid = fork();
    if (pid == 0) x = 2;
    else wait(NULL);
    printf("%s: &x=%p x=%d\n", pid == 0 ? "child" : "parent", (void *)&x, x);
    return 0;
}
```

Fill in the blank. This toy MMU uses a single-level table of 4 KiB pages, and each table entry is just the physical page number. With `pt[3] = 7`, what does `translate(pt, 0x3ABC)` return?

```c
uint32_t translate(const uint32_t *pt, uint32_t va) {
    uint32_t vpn = va >> 12;
    uint32_t ppn = pt[vpn];
    return (ppn << ____) | (va & 0xFFF);
}
```
